{"authored" [{"question" "q-117278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${f}^{′}(1)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/state.json" "file_sha256" "def1579efa9e17ed2eeb15ff956d4dffcaf647b3ec61664fe665a85b779303bf" "basis" 1238 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{a}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $-{x}^{3}$ and $x.$\n\nThe integrand ${t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(-3{x}^{2}){u}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{(-{x}^{3})}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{x}^{3})+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{x}^{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=-{x}^{3}.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{1}^{3}+{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/state.json" "file_sha256" "def1579efa9e17ed2eeb15ff956d4dffcaf647b3ec61664fe665a85b779303bf" "basis" 1238 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b65df1969efc25f29516062e2caa1a3c57e4f6191012bead8afceea49150b42e" "basis" 1238 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/state.json" "file_sha256" "def1579efa9e17ed2eeb15ff956d4dffcaf647b3ec61664fe665a85b779303bf" "basis" 1238 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/edb-import/verification.json"} {"ques