/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-116475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-116475" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-116475" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2{e}^{2}"] ["math" "4{e}^{2}"] ["math" "8e"] ["math" "e"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-116475" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{e}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-30627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-5}^{x}(a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}s$\nfor $x>-5,$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-30627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1$ is continuous on $[-5,x],$ where $x>-5.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-5}^{x}(a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1)\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}t & =a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-30627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-30627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-30627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1"] ["math" "3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+1"] ["math" "3{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+1"] ["math" "a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+1"] ["math" "a{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-30627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{3}^{x}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t$ for $x>3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{t}^{2}+4}$ is continuous on $[3,x],$ where $x>3.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{3}^{x}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-4552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3}{13}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3}{13}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3}{13}"] ["math" "\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(t)=t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}.$ Find ${g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ given the function $g$ is defined by $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(t)=t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}$ is continuous on $[0,x],$ where $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{x}t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =x\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-75583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0"] ["math" "{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1"] ["math" "{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt[3]{2}}{4}"] ["math" "{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-30618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-1}^{x}({t}^{2}-2t+5)\\,\\text{d}t$\nfor $x>-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-30618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function ${t}^{2}-2t+5$ is continuous on $[-1,x]$ where $x>-1.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-1}^{x}({t}^{2}-2t+5)\\,\\text{d}t & ={x}^{2}-2x+5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-30618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-30618" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-30618" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+2"] ["math" "2x+5x-2"] ["math" "2x-2"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+2x+5"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-2x+5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-30618" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}-2x+5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-332071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(t)=2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1.$ Find ${h}^{′}(0)$ given the function $h$ is defined by $h(x)={\\int }_{x}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $x<1.$\n\n${h}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-332071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\int }_{x}^{1}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{1}^{x}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{1}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{1}^{x}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{1}^{x}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${h}^{′}(0),$ we substitute $x=0$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(0) & =-(2\\cdot {0}^{3}+{0}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-(0+0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-332071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-332071" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-332071" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-316389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(t)={t}^{2}{e}^{3t}.$ Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ given the function $h$ is defined by $h(x)={\\int }_{x}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $x<3.$\n\n${h}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-316389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we prefer the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\int }_{x}^{3}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{3}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{3}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{3}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{3}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${h}^{′}(2),$ we substitute $x=2$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =-{2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{3(2)} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-316389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-316389" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-316389" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4e^6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-316373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $x<2,$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-316373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here, the variable limit $x$ appears as the lower limit of integration. To apply FTC II directly, we prefer the variable on the upper limit. Using the property of definite integrals, we rewrite\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow, by FTC II we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-316373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-316373" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-316373" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\sin(x^2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-332067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Find ${h}^{′}(\\pi )$ given the function $h$ is defined by $h(x)={\\int }_{x}^{2\\pi }f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n${h}^{′}(\\pi )=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-332067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we prefer the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\int }_{x}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{2\\pi }^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{2\\pi }^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{2\\pi }^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${h}^{′}(\\pi ),$ we substitute $x=\\pi $ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-332067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-332067" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-332067" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+7t+12}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t$\nfor $x<0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{x}^{0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+7t+12}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{t}^{2}+7t+12}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+7t+12}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{t}^{2}+7t+12}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{t}^{2}+7t+12}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+12}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-75217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+4)"] ["math" "-(x+4)"] ["math" "-(x+5)"] ["math" "-\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+12}{x-2}"] ["math" "\\frac{2x+7}{x-2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75217" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+12}{x-2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${g}^{′}(x)$ if $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute ${g}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={x}^{3}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[0,u].$ Therefore, by FTC II, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({u}^{3}-2u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})\\cdot (({x}^{3})^{3}-2({x}^{3})) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot ({x}^{9}-2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}({x}^{9}-2{x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-333573" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333573" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3x^{11}-6x^5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${f}^{′}(2)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{2}^{2x}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=2x$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{2x}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{2}^{u}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[2,u],$ and so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\sqrt{{u}^{3}-u} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)\\cdot \\sqrt{(2x)^{3}-(2x)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{8{x}^{3}-2x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${f}^{′}(2),$ we substitute $x=2$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =2\\sqrt{8(2)^{3}-2(2)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{64-4} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{15}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-4553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{15}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{15}"] ["math" "8\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\sqrt{15}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For $x>0,$ find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=-{x}^{2}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of the definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{-4}^{u}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{-4}^{u}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{-4}^{u}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u}) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-4776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{x}"] ["math" "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] ["math" "-x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-4776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-316401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${g}^{′}(x)$ for $g(x)={\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-316401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute ${g}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={e}^{x}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{5}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{5}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{5}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡({u}^{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({e}^{x})^{3})) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{3x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-316401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-316401" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-316401" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-e^x\\cos(e^{3x})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${g}^{′}(x)$ if $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}+1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}} ${e}^{{x}^{3}+1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute ${g}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={x}^{3}+1$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}+1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[0,u].$ Therefore, by FTC II, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {e}^{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\,{e}^{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-333563" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333563" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3x^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-117278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${f}^{′}(1)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-117278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{a}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $-{x}^{3}$ and $x.$\n\nThe integrand ${t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(-3{x}^{2}){u}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{(-{x}^{3})}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{x}^{3})+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{x}^{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=-{x}^{3}.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{1}^{3}+{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-117278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-117278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-117278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡1"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-117278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $G(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,$ then ${G}^{′}(0)=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).$\n\nThe integrand ${e}^{t}$ is continuous for all $t.$ Therefore, applying the FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot {e}^{u} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${G}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{′}(0) & =-{e}^{0}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)} \\\\  & =-1-0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-333952" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333952" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $G(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,$ then ${G}^{′}(0)=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x).$\n\nThe integrand $(1+{t}^{2})^{2}$ is continuous for all $t.$ Therefore, applying the FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-(1+{x}^{2})^{2}+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {(1+{u}^{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-(1+{x}^{2})^{2}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\cdot {(1+{tan}^{2}⁡(x))}^{2} \\\\  & =-(1+{x}^{2})^{2}+{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\cdot {(1+{tan}^{2}⁡(x))}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x).$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${G}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{′}(0) & =-(1+{0}^{2})^{2}+{sec}^{2}⁡(0)\\cdot {(1+{tan}^{2}⁡(0))}^{2} \\\\  & =-1+1\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-333882" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-333882" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-117289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${f}^{′}(1)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-117289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nThe integrand $\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}+3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}((\\sqrt{x})^{4}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}({1}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-117289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-117289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-117289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{4}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-117289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{8}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${F}^{′}(e)$ if $F(x)={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{a}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $x.$\n\nThe integrand $\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{du}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{u}{2+{u}^{2}})+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}})+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2x+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x).$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${F}^{′}(e).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡e}{2e+e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2e+e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{3e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ee31d7d65e6fb8bc30e202a7a4a13e3d3457fc2c54686b5af8609f9889c9d3c1"} {"question" "q-75458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{3e}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{3e}"] ["math" "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "q-75458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{3e}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1779c07ebd3babf761669df25b5994b79c4dccb8b0fca57beaa0f775193374e"} {"question" "e-438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_2^x \\dfrac {t^3} {t-1}\\,\\textrm d t$ for $x>2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/example-438.json" "file_sha256" "f6931d11e798f4a1b70c2346703ae969637eedc7a860081564d1d8b0a797928c"} {"question" "e-438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $\\dfrac {t^3} {t-1}$ is continuous on $[2,x],$ where $x>2.$ So FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\frac{{t}^{3}}{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/example-438.json" "file_sha256" "f6931d11e798f4a1b70c2346703ae969637eedc7a860081564d1d8b0a797928c"} {"question" "e-439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_x^0  \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1}\\,\\textrm d t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/example-439.json" "file_sha256" "b9993322bd6285d1550abd4afbbe9103aeb1300151b2b153a5fdef2744a4304a"} {"question" "e-439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_x^0 \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t  = -  \\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n \\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_x^0  \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \n\t& \\, \\, = \\, \\, \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\left(-\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \\right)\\\n\t& \\, \\, = \\, \\, -\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\left(\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \\right)\\\n\t&= \\, \\, -\\dfrac{x^3}{x^2 +1}.}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/example-439.json" "file_sha256" "b9993322bd6285d1550abd4afbbe9103aeb1300151b2b153a5fdef2744a4304a"} {"question" "e-440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $f'(x)$ for $f(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_2^{x^2}\\arctan t\\,\\textrm d t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/example-440.json" "file_sha256" "dd86ad1fa045d2d6d5d527b057aee64b1eea71e32eb8448f4f9a8fdc5ed9ed04"} {"question" "e-440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute $f'(x),$  we first make the substitution $u=x^2$  and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{du}}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{2}^{u}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe integrand is continuous for all $t,$ so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/example-440.json" "file_sha256" "dd86ad1fa045d2d6d5d527b057aee64b1eea71e32eb8448f4f9a8fdc5ed9ed04"} {"question" "e-441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $f'(1)$ if  $\\displaystyle f(x) = \\int_x^{x^2} (t^2 + 2t)  \\,\\, \\textrm d t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/example-441.json" "file_sha256" "bf3cf1e869c018f546d35be492851d25e84cf4970237ad22622f92a1cf7959e5"} {"question" "e-441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $x^2.$\n\nThe integrand $t^2 + 2t$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x+2x({u}^{2}+2u) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x+2x(({x}^{2})^{2}+2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x+2x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x+2{x}^{5}+4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=x^2,$ as before.\n\nFinally, we can compute $f'(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)^{5}+4(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =2+4-1-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043209/example-441.json" "file_sha256" "bf3cf1e869c018f546d35be492851d25e84cf4970237ad22622f92a1cf7959e5"}] "usage" {"q-117278" [] "e-441" [] "q-4552" [] "e-440" [] "e-439" [] "e-438" []} "basis" 1600 "retractions" [[17592186078858 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{d}{dx}∫_{3}^{x}\\frac{\\cos t}{t^{2} + 4}dt$ for $x > 3$."] [17592186078233 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_2^x \\dfrac {t^3} {t-1}\\,\\textrm d t$ for $x>2$."] [17592186078233 :question/worked-solution "The function\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {t^3} {t-1}\n$$\n\nis continuous on $[2,x]$, where $x>2$. So FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{dx}∫_{2}^{x}\\frac{t^{3}}{t - 1}dt = \\frac{x^{3}}{x - 1}\n$$"] [17592186078234 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_x^0 \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1}\\,\\textrm d t$."] [17592186078234 :question/worked-solution "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_x^0 \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t =-\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_x^0 \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t & \\, \\, = \\, \\, \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\left(-\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \\right) \\\\ & \\, \\, = \\, \\, -\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\left(\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \\right) \\\\ &= \\, \\, -\\dfrac{x^3}{x^2 +1}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186078235 :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ for $f(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_2^{x^2}\\arctan t\\,\\textrm d t$."] [17592186078235 :question/worked-solution "To compute $f'(x)$, we first make the substitution $u=x^2$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}∫_{2}^{x^{2}}\\arctan tdt \\\\\n&= \\frac{du}{dx} \\cdot \\frac{d}{du}∫_{2}^{u}\\arctan tdt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous for all $t$, so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{du}{dx} \\cdot \\arctan (u) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\cdot \\arctan (u) \\\\\n&= 2x\\arctan (x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186078236 :question/problem "Find $f'(1)$ if $\\displaystyle f(x) = \\int_x^{x^2} (t^2 + 2t) \\,\\, \\textrm d t$."] [17592186078236 :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{x}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt &= ∫_{x}^{a}(t^{2} + 2t)dt + ∫_{a}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt \\\\\n&= - ∫_{a}^{x}(t^{2} + 2t)dt + ∫_{a}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $x^2$.\n\nThe integrand $t^2 + 2t$ is continuous for all $t$, so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(- ∫_{a}^{x}(t^{2} + 2t)dt + ∫_{a}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{d}{dx}(∫_{a}^{x}(t^{2} + 2t)dt) + \\frac{d}{dx}(∫_{a}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt) \\\\\n&= - (x^{2} + 2x) + \\frac{du}{dx}\\frac{d}{du}(∫_{a}^{u}(t^{2} + 2t)dt) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} - 2x + 2x(u^{2} + 2u) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} - 2x + 2x((x^{2})^{2} + 2x^{2}) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} - 2x + 2x(x^{4} + 2x^{2}) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} - 2x + 2x^{5} + 4x^{3} \\\\\n&= 2x^{5} + 4x^{3} - x^{2} - 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=x^2$, as before.\n\nFinally, we can compute $f'(1)$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(1) &= 2(1)^{5} + 4(1)^{3} - (1)^{2} - 2(1) \\\\\n&= 2 + 4 - 1 - 2 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186076360 17592186076361 17592186076362 17592186076363 17592186076364 17592186294485 17592186294486 17592186294487 17592186294488 17592186294489] "immutable_fields" [[17592186076359 :answer-field/correct] [17592186076359 :answer-field/id] [17592186076359 :answer-field/key] [17592186076359 :answer-field/type] [17592186294484 :answer-field/correct] [17592186294484 :answer-field/id] [17592186294484 :answer-field/key] [17592186294484 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
